{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend underpricing: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rising regime, the model systematically under-forecasts levels (often by ~+2 to +5 €/t). Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small at days 1–5, larger at days 10–30).",
    "Momentum/lag error: forecasts react too slowly to regime changes; true prices continue in the direction of the recent move more than the model implies. Add short-term momentum: if 5-day return > 0, tilt days 1–10 upward; if < 0, tilt downward (smaller magnitude than the uptrend case).",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: the model pulls back toward the recent average, but realized outcomes persist at higher levels during rallies. Reduce mean reversion strength in rising markets; keep stronger mean reversion only in flat/range regimes.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: error grows with horizon (days 15–30 are most biased low in the examples). Use a horizon-weighted correction (e.g., +0.1 to +0.2 €/t per forecast day during bullish regimes).",
    "Post-spike behavior: after sharp upward moves, the model tends to fade the move too quickly (predicting pullbacks that don’t materialize). After a positive jump day, suppress downward corrections for ~5–10 days and keep forecasts closer to the elevated level."
  ]
}
